@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    PAY-540  Refund tests are flaky since the production seed was
             loaded into the test database. Three things to fix
             before the release branch cuts at 17:00.

Four files, open as four buffers. `:bn` moves to the next. Write each
file with `:w` before you leave it and finish with `:wqa`.

  1. refund.test.ts — the assertion library is changing. Every
     `expect(a).toEqual(b);` becomes `assert.equal(a, b);`. The
     `rejects.toThrow` and `toHaveBeenCalled` assertions have no
     equivalent yet and stay exactly as they are.

  2. fixtures.ts — the fixture order ids collide with the production
     seed, which uses `ord_9101` upwards. Add 1000 to every order id
     in the file. Customer ids and refund ids are not in the seed and
     do not move.

  3. refund.constants.ts — the tests already use the upper-case
     status codes, and this file still has them lower case. Upper-case
     every status code: in the union, the constants, and the keys of
     the messages map. The message *text* is read by customers and
     stays as written.

  4. seed.sql — the production seed, open so you can see the
     collision. It is not yours to edit.

Every tool is from this chapter. Which one, per file, is the job.

@keys
| `\v…\((.*)\)…\((.*)\)…` | groups around the two arguments, literal parens escaped |
| `\1` `\2` | the groups, in the replacement |
| `\zs` | start the match after the context |
| `\=submatch(0)+1000` | the match as a number, plus a thousand |
| `\U\1` and `\U&` | a group or the match, upper-cased |
| `:w` then `:bn` | save, next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write every changed file and quit |

@hints
Test file: `expect\((.*)\)\.toEqual\((.*)\);` captures the two
halves. The `toThrow` lines do not contain `toEqual` so the pattern
cannot touch them. Write `assert.equal(\1, \2);` as the replacement.
--
Fixtures: `ord_\zs\d+` makes the digits the match and `ord_` the
context, so `\=submatch(0)+1000` only sees the number. The `g` flag
matters — one line holds four ids.
--
Constants: the codes are the only single-quoted single words in the
file, so `'(\w+)'` with `'\U\1'` upper-cases them and cannot match a
sentence. The map keys are a second, smaller substitute: a word at the
start of an indented line, followed by a colon.
--
If any of the three patterns worries you, the `n` flag will tell you
how many hits it has before it changes anything, and the `c` flag
will show you each one.

@solution
**refund.test.ts** —
`:%s/\vexpect\((.*)\)\.toEqual\((.*)\);/assert.equal(\1, \2);/`.
Six lines restructured, the `toThrow` and `toHaveBeenCalled` lines
untouched because the pattern names `toEqual`. `:w`, `:bn`.

**fixtures.ts** — `:%s/\vord_\zs\d+/\=submatch(0)+1000/g`. Eight
ids shifted; the `cus_` and `rfd_` ids are not `ord_` and were never
in play. `:w`, `:bn`.

**refund.constants.ts** — two commands: `:%s/\v'(\w+)'/'\U\1'/g` for
the quoted codes, then `:%s/\v^  \zs\w+\ze:/\U&/` for the map keys.
The messages are multi-word strings and match neither. `:wqa`.

**seed.sql** — nothing. `:wqa` writes only what changed.

Around a hundred and seventy keystrokes across four files, and not one
of them was a character of the result typed by hand. Everything was
described: the shape of an assertion, the digits after `ord_`, a
single word in quotes.

This is what the chapter was for. A substitute is a small program:
a pattern that picks the text apart, a replacement that puts it back
together, and a range and flags that say where and how carefully.
Backreferences, expressions and case modifiers are what give the
replacement side something to say. The judgement is in the pattern —
`toEqual` rather than `expect`, `ord_` rather than `\d+`, a single
quoted word rather than any quoted string — and in knowing, before
you press Enter on `%`, that you have seen or counted every hit.
Chapter 15 hands you `:g`, which runs a command on every line a
pattern matches, and a substitute is the command it most often runs.

@start refund.test.ts
import { RefundProvider } from './refund.provider';
import { FIXTURES } from './fixtures';

describe('RefundProvider', () => {
  it('approves a pending refund', async () => {
    const refund = await provider.approve(FIXTURES.pending.id);

    expect(refund.status).toEqual('APPROVED');
    expect(refund.approvedBy).toEqual('reviewer_1');
  });

  it('settles an approved refund', async () => {
    const refund = await provider.settle(FIXTURES.approved.id);

    expect(refund.status).toEqual('SETTLED');
    expect(refund.settledAmount).toEqual(1999);
    expect(paymentService.refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith(FIXTURES.approved.orderId, 1999);
  });

  it('lists refunds for an order', async () => {
    const refunds = await provider.findByOrder(FIXTURES.approved.orderId);

    expect(refunds.map((refund) => refund.id)).toEqual(['rfd_0301', 'rfd_0302']);
    expect(refunds.length).toEqual(2);
  });

  it('refuses to settle a rejected refund', async () => {
    await expect(provider.settle(FIXTURES.rejected.id)).rejects.toThrow('REFUND_NOT_APPROVED');
  });

  it('refuses an unknown refund', async () => {
    await expect(provider.approve('rfd_0000')).rejects.toThrow('REFUND_NOT_FOUND');
    expect(paymentService.refund).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});

@expected refund.test.ts
import { RefundProvider } from './refund.provider';
import { FIXTURES } from './fixtures';

describe('RefundProvider', () => {
  it('approves a pending refund', async () => {
    const refund = await provider.approve(FIXTURES.pending.id);

    assert.equal(refund.status, 'APPROVED');
    assert.equal(refund.approvedBy, 'reviewer_1');
  });

  it('settles an approved refund', async () => {
    const refund = await provider.settle(FIXTURES.approved.id);

    assert.equal(refund.status, 'SETTLED');
    assert.equal(refund.settledAmount, 1999);
    expect(paymentService.refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith(FIXTURES.approved.orderId, 1999);
  });

  it('lists refunds for an order', async () => {
    const refunds = await provider.findByOrder(FIXTURES.approved.orderId);

    assert.equal(refunds.map((refund) => refund.id), ['rfd_0301', 'rfd_0302']);
    assert.equal(refunds.length, 2);
  });

  it('refuses to settle a rejected refund', async () => {
    await expect(provider.settle(FIXTURES.rejected.id)).rejects.toThrow('REFUND_NOT_APPROVED');
  });

  it('refuses an unknown refund', async () => {
    await expect(provider.approve('rfd_0000')).rejects.toThrow('REFUND_NOT_FOUND');
    expect(paymentService.refund).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});

@start fixtures.ts
export const FIXTURES = {
  pending: { id: 'rfd_0301', orderId: 'ord_9101', customerId: 'cus_9101', amount: 1999 },
  approved: { id: 'rfd_0302', orderId: 'ord_9104', customerId: 'cus_9104', amount: 1999 },
  rejected: { id: 'rfd_0303', orderId: 'ord_9112', customerId: 'cus_9112', amount: 4500 },
  settled: { id: 'rfd_0304', orderId: 'ord_9118', customerId: 'cus_9118', amount: 750 },
};

export const ORDER_IDS = ['ord_9101', 'ord_9104', 'ord_9112', 'ord_9118'];

